import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The ai package, a plugin: Claude from an app's server. Installing it (the key, the model),
## asking, streaming an answer to a page, typed answers, the app's functions as tools, agents (and
## agents as tools), conversations, what it costs, what can go wrong, what it sends, and the example
## app, file by file. Static: asking needs a server.
meta = {
	title: "AI",
	description: "packages/ai, a plugin for Roc Solid's server: Claude through Anthropic's Messages API, in plain Roc. An answer whole or streamed to a page as it is written, an answer read into a Roc type, a model that uses the app's own functions as tools until it is done, and agents that other agents use as tools.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "ask", label: "Asking" },
		{ id: "stream", label: "Streaming to a page" },
		{ id: "extract", label: "Typed answers" },
		{ id: "tools", label: "Tools" },
		{ id: "agents", label: "Agents" },
		{ id: "conversations", label: "Conversations" },
		{ id: "cost", label: "What it costs" },
		{ id: "problems", label: "What went wrong" },
		{ id: "sends", label: "What it sends" },
		{ id: "example", label: "The example app" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/ai</code>]}
				title="Claude, from your app's server"
			>
				Ask a question and get the answer whole, or stream it to a page as the model writes it. Read an
				answer into a Roc type. Give the model your app's own functions as tools, and it uses them until it
				is done. Make agents, each a model with its own role and tools, and let one hand work to the others.
				The package is plain Roc on the server platform's HTTP client: no SDK, and no change to the platform.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				The package does the parts that are easy to get wrong. It puts a stream's events back together into
				the model's message, with its thinking and signatures exactly as they came, because the API checks
				them when a conversation goes on. It tries a rate limit, an overloaded API or a passing server error
				again after the time the API asks for, but never once text has gone to a page. A refusal is an error the app must handle, not empty text. When
				the page that asked goes, the request ends and the model stops writing. A Roc type becomes a JSON
				Schema the API's structured outputs accept, and each turn's tokens go on the log. Your app keeps its
				prompts, its tools, and what it does with the answers.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in the app's header, with the two packages it uses (<code>crypto</code> and
				<code>tools</code>), and list its <a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugin</a>. The package
				names the server platform, so only the server's half of the app sees it. This is the example app's
				<code>main.roc</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.ai_example_main.file} source={Sources.ai_example_main.rocx} />
			<p>
				Then give the server an API key, from <a href="https://console.anthropic.com">console.anthropic.com</a>,
				as <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code>. Outside <code>--dev</code> the server doesn't start without one. Under
				<code>--dev</code> (as <code>rocx dev</code> starts it) it starts, and each call answers <code>NoKey</code>.
			</p>
			<p>The model it asks:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Model</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>by default</td><td><code>claude-opus-5-5</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td>under <code>--dev</code> (<code>rocx dev</code>)</td><td><code>claude-haiku-5-5</code>, so answers come fast and cost little while you develop</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>ANTHROPIC_MODEL</code> or the plugin's <code>model</code> names one</td><td>that model, under <code>--dev</code> too</td></tr>
						<tr><td>a request names one (<code>model</code>)</td><td>that model, for that call</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>Once started, the server logs which model it asks, and where (<code>ai.model</code>):</p>
			<Code source={"claude-haiku-5-5 (the model under --dev: ANTHROPIC_MODEL or the plugin's model names another), at https://api.anthropic.com"} plain numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Each setting can be given to <code>{"Ai.plugin({ key, model, base_url })"}</code> instead of the
				environment. An option given in code wins.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Environment</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>key</code></td><td><code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code></td><td>none: outside <code>--dev</code> the server doesn't start</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>model</code></td><td><code>ANTHROPIC_MODEL</code></td><td><code>claude-opus-5-5</code>, or <code>claude-haiku-5-5</code> under <code>--dev</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>base_url</code></td><td><code>ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL</code></td><td><code>https://api.anthropic.com</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL</code> is where the API is. The example's tests point it at their stand-in for
				the API.
			</p>

			<Heading id="ask">Asking</Heading>
			<p>
				Every call takes an <code>Ai.Request</code>: a record whose fields all have defaults, but which needs
				one of <code>prompt</code> or <code>messages</code>.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>prompt</code></td><td><code>""</code></td><td>the user's message, after <code>messages</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>messages</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>the conversation so far: an earlier reply's <code>messages</code>, or <code>Message.load</code>ed</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>system</code></td><td><code>""</code></td><td>how the model should go about it: its role, the rules, the app's facts</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>model</code></td><td>the plugin's</td><td>this call's model</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>max_tokens</code></td><td><code>64_000</code></td><td>how much the model may write, its thinking too</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>effort</code></td><td><code>Medium</code></td><td><code>Low</code>, <code>Medium</code>, <code>High</code>, <code>XHigh</code> or <code>Max</code>: how hard it thinks. More costs more and takes longer</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>cache</code></td><td><code>Bool.True</code></td><td>keep what is sent in the API's prompt cache, so the next call that starts the same reads it for a tenth of the price</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>max_turns</code></td><td><code>10</code></td><td>how many turns <code>run!</code> takes at most</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>label</code></td><td><code>""</code></td><td>what the call is, on the log (<code>ai.turn</code>'s <code>label</code>: <code>"summary"</code>, an agent's name)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>Low</code> effort suits a quick answer, such as a chat or a label; <code>High</code> and above,
				work that needs care. <code>Ai.ask!</code> waits for the whole answer. The example sums up the notes
				with it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.ai_example_summary.file} source={Sources.ai_example_summary.rocx} />
			<p>The reply is <code>{"{ text, messages, stop, usage, model }"}</code>:</p>
			<ul>
				<li><code>text</code>: the answer's text;</li>
				<li><code>messages</code>: the conversation with the answer in it, to go on with or to keep;</li>
				<li>
					<code>stop</code>: why the model stopped: <code>EndTurn</code>, <code>MaxTokens</code> when the text is
					cut off, <code>ToolUse</code>, and a few more;
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>usage</code>: what it cost, in tokens: <code>{"{ input, output, cache_read, cache_write }"}</code>
					(all of <code>run!</code>'s turns, for <code>run!</code>);
				</li>
				<li><code>model</code>: the model that wrote it.</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="stream">Streaming to a page</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>{"Ai.stream!(request, heard!)"}</code> gives <code>heard!</code> each piece of text as the model
				writes it, then answers the whole reply. From an endpoint's
				<a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}><code>events!</code></a>, each piece is an event the page listens to
				with <code>Endpoint.listen!</code>:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="+server.roc, in part"
				source={"events! = |{ params, .. }|\n\tOk(Events.stream(|{ send!, .. }|\n\t\tmatch Ai.stream!({ prompt: params.question }, |text| send!({ text: text })) {\n\t\t\tOk(_) => send!({ text: \"\" })\n\t\t\tErr(Stopped(Gone)) => Err(Gone)\n\t\t\tErr(problem) => send!({ text: Ai.explain(problem) })\n\t\t}))"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<Callout kind="note" title="When the page goes, the model stops">
				<p>
					When the page goes, <code>send!</code> answers <code>Err(Gone)</code> and <code>heard!</code> passes it
					on. The package closes the request to the API, so the model stops writing, and stops costing.
					<code>stream!</code> answers <code>Err(Stopped(Gone))</code>, which <code>events!</code> hands back as
					<code>Err(Gone)</code>.
				</p>
			</Callout>
			<p>
				<code>events!</code> takes no body. So a page posts what it asks first, then listens to the events of
				a path that names it. The example's chat posts the user's message to <code>/api/chat/[chat]</code>,
				then listens to the same path's events:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.ai_example_listen.file}, in part"} source={Sources.ai_example_listen.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>heard!</code> is given <code>Event(said)</code> for each event, then <code>Ended</code>, or
				<code>Failed(problem)</code>. On the server, <code>post!</code> adds the message to the chat's
				conversation, kept in <a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a> under the chat's id, and <code>events!</code>
				answers it with the example's assistant (an <a href="#agents">agent</a>, asked with
				<code>Agent.chat!</code>): its text as it is written, the name of each agent or tool as it is about to
				be used, then the notes as they are now.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.ai_example_chat.file} source={Sources.ai_example_chat.rocx} />

			<Heading id="extract">Typed answers</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>{"Ai.extract!(request, Ai.schema(tags))"}</code> holds the model to the JSON Schema of the answer's
				type (the API's structured outputs) and reads what it writes into that type with <code>Json</code>,
				as an endpoint reads a body. The type comes from the annotation:
			</p>
			<Code
				source={"Meal : { items : List({ food : Str, grams : U32, kcal : U32 }), total_kcal : U32 }\n\nmeal : Meal\nmeal = Ai.extract!({ system: \"Read the meal into its foods.\", prompt: said }, Ai.schema([]))?"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>The example's meal endpoint answers with that type, read from what the user typed:</p>
			<Code title={Sources.ai_example_meal.file} source={Sources.ai_example_meal.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The schema is the platform's</strong>, the one the
					<a href={"${Paths.routing}#openapi"}>OpenAPI document</a> uses. <code>tags</code> names the tags of the
					type's unions (<code>["Breakfast", "Lunch"]</code>; <code>[]</code> for a type with none), because a
					union's parser doesn't say them.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>It is made the API's subset on the way</strong>: every object closed
					(<code>additionalProperties: false</code>), <code>oneOf</code> written as <code>anyOf</code>, and what
					the API doesn't hold a model to (an integer's range, a string's length) left out. <code>Json</code>
					checks those as the answer is read, so an answer outside a <code>U32</code> is
					<code>InvalidJson</code>, not a wrong number.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A type that isn't a record</strong> is asked for as an object's <code>result</code> field.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Ai.schema</code> is given as a value</strong>, so it is made where the type is known, as
					<code>{"McpHttp.openapi({})"}</code> is.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				An answer that reaches <code>max_tokens</code> before its JSON ends is <code>Truncated</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="tools">Tools</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>{"Ai.run!(request, tools, context, heard!)"}</code> sends the model the tools. Each turn that asks
				for some has them run, in order, each given <code>context</code>, and their answers sent back, until the
				model stops asking, or <code>max_turns</code> turns have gone (<code>TooManyTurns</code>).
				<code>heard!</code> is given <code>Text(piece)</code> as the model writes, and <code>Calling(name)</code>
				as it is about to use a tool.
			</p>
			<p>
				A tool is <code>packages/tools</code>' <code>Tool</code>, which the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_mcp}>MCP
				package</a> serves too, so one tool serves the app's own model and its MCP server alike.
				<code>Ai.tool</code> makes one from a function of the app's: its name and what it does, for the model;
				the function, given the arguments as its type and the context; and the arguments' schema, the
				platform's. The example's notes, and the three tools its notes keeper uses:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.ai_example_notes.file} source={Sources.ai_example_notes.rocx} />
			<p>
				<code>McpHttp.endpoint</code>'s, <code>McpHttp.tool</code>'s and <code>Tool.typed</code>'s tools work too,
				with their own context (an MCP tool's is <code>McpHttp.Caller(c)</code>: the request, the context,
				<code>progress!</code>). What the model gets back:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>the tool's answer, as JSON text;</li>
				<li>its <code>Err(why)</code>, as a tool error, which the model can read and act on;</li>
				<li>
					arguments that don't fit the tool's type, input that isn't JSON, and a tool it doesn't have, as tool
					errors too;
				</li>
				<li>
					a failure inside (<code>Failed</code>), as <code>{"\"<name> failed.\""}</code>, its reason logged
					(<code>ai.tool</code>) and not sent.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Each tool's input is streamed as the model writes it, then read whole and checked against its type
				before the tool runs. A turn cut off by <code>max_tokens</code>, or refused, never runs its tools.
			</p>

			<Heading id="agents">Agents</Heading>
			<p>
				An <code>Agent(ctx)</code> is a model with a role and its tools, made once as a plain value and asked as
				often as needed:
			</p>
			<Code
				source={"keeper : Agent({})\nkeeper = {\n\tname: \"notes_keeper\",\n\tdescription: \"Keeps the user's notes. Give it a task in words: add a note, say what is noted, or remove one.\",\n\tsystem: \"You keep the user's notes, with the tools list_notes, add_note and remove_note.\",\n\ttools: Notes.tools,\n\teffort: Low,\n}\n\nreply = Agent.ask!(keeper, \"What did I note about the dentist?\", {}, |_| Ok({}))?\nreply = Agent.chat!(keeper, conversation, {}, heard!)?"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>name</code>, <code>description</code></td><td>what a model that uses the agent as a tool reads: its name, and what it does</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>system</code></td><td>its role: how it goes about its work</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>tools</code></td><td>the tools it may use (<code>[]</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>model</code></td><td>its model (the plugin's)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>effort</code></td><td>how hard it thinks (<code>Medium</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>max_turns</code>, <code>max_tokens</code></td><td>as a request's (10, and 64,000)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>{"Agent.ask!(agent, prompt, context, heard!)"}</code> is a conversation of one message, and
				<code>{"Agent.chat!(agent, messages, context, heard!)"}</code> the next turn of one, the user's message
				last. Both are <code>Ai.run!</code> with the agent's settings: <code>heard!</code> is given its text and
				the tools it is about to use, and its turns are on the log under its name (<code>label</code>).
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Agents as tools.</strong> <code>agent.as_tool()</code> is the agent as a <code>Tool</code>: its
				name and description are the tool's, and it takes one argument, <code>task</code>, the work it is
				handed, in words. So a team is plain values: specialists, each with its own prompt and tools, and an
				agent that knows when to ask which. The calling agent stays in charge, as in Strands Agents' agents as
				tools. The example's chat talks to such a team:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.ai_example_team.file} source={Sources.ai_example_team.rocx} />
			<p>
				The chat's <code>events!</code> (under <a href="#stream">streaming to a page</a>) asks
				<code>Team.assistant</code> with <code>Agent.chat!</code>, the chat's conversation, and a
				<code>heard!</code> that sends each piece to the page.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A task is a conversation of its own.</strong> The agent sees nothing of the conversation it was
					called from, so the calling model must put everything needed in the task. Say so in its system
					prompt, as the assistant's does.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>An agent's own work isn't streamed.</strong> Its caller hears <code>Calling(name)</code>, then
					the answer. The example's page shows the assistant's text, and while an agent works, its name. The
					agent's turns are on the log under its name.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Its answer is the tool's result.</strong> The agent's final text goes back to the calling
					model. What went wrong is a tool error (<code>{"\"<name> couldn't do it: …\""}</code>), and so is a
					call with no task, without asking the API.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One context.</strong> Every agent in a team gives its tools the context it was given.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>An MCP tool too.</strong> Being a <code>Tool</code>, an agent can be an MCP server's tool as well.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="conversations">Conversations</Heading>
			<p>
				A conversation is a <code>List(Message)</code>. <code>Message.user(text)</code> is the user's message.
				The model's are kept as the API wrote them, thinking blocks and their signatures included, because the
				API needs them back. <code>reply.messages</code> is the conversation after a call: give it as the next
				request's <code>messages</code>, with a new <code>prompt</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				<code>Message.save(messages)</code> writes a conversation as JSON text, to keep in Kv or a table, and
				<code>Message.load(text)</code> reads it back exactly. <code>Message.text(message)</code> is a message's
				text, without its thinking or its tools' use. The example keeps each chat's conversation in Kv for a
				day:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.ai_example_chats.file} source={Sources.ai_example_chats.rocx} />
			<p>
				After a server-side fallback in the middle of a turn (another model took over where the first
				declined, see <a href="#sends">what it sends</a>), the first model's thinking and tool uses before the
				<code>fallback</code> block are left out of the message, as the API asks. The <code>fallback</code> block
				stays where it was.
			</p>

			<Heading id="cost">What it costs</Heading>
			<p>Each turn is on the log, with its tokens (<code>ai.turn</code>):</p>
			<Code
				source={"{\"msg\":\"ai.turn\",\"label\":\"notes_keeper\",\"model\":\"claude-haiku-5-5\",\"stop\":\"tool_use\",\"input\":4,\"cache_read\":640,\"cache_write\":215,\"output\":112,\"ms\":878}"}
				plain
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>label</code>: the request's, or an agent's name, so what each agent spends can be told apart
					(left out when there is none);
				</li>
				<li><code>input</code>: what was read without the cache;</li>
				<li><code>cache_read</code>: what came from the cache, at a tenth of the price;</li>
				<li><code>cache_write</code>: what was written to the cache, at a quarter more, once;</li>
				<li><code>output</code>: what the model wrote; <code>ms</code>: how long the turn took.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				With <code>cache</code> on (the default), a conversation's next turn reads all of the last one from the
				cache. A prompt shorter than the model's minimum (512 to 4,096 tokens, by model) isn't cached, and
				costs nothing more for asking. The other levers are the model and <code>effort</code>:
				<code>--dev</code> asks <code>claude-haiku-5-5</code>, and the example asks with <code>Low</code>
				effort.
			</p>

			<Heading id="problems">What went wrong</Heading>
			<p>
				A call that fails answers an <code>Ai.Problem(err)</code>, and <code>Ai.explain(problem)</code> says it in
				a sentence, for the log or a page:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Tag</th><th>When</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>NoKey</code></td><td>no <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> (under <code>--dev</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Status(code, why)</code></td><td>the API refused: 400 the request, 401 the key, 429 too many (still, after two tries), 529 overloaded</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Unreachable(why)</code>, <code>TimedOut</code>, <code>Broken(why)</code></td><td>no whole answer came</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Refused(category)</code></td><td>the model declined (the API's category, as <code>"cyber"</code>; often <code>""</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Truncated</code></td><td><code>extract!</code>'s answer reached <code>max_tokens</code> before its JSON ended</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>InvalidJson(why)</code></td><td><code>extract!</code>'s answer didn't fit its type</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>TooManyTurns</code></td><td><code>run!</code> took <code>max_turns</code> turns and the model still wanted its tools</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stopped(err)</code></td><td><code>heard!</code> answered <code>Err(err)</code>: from an endpoint's <code>events!</code>, the page went (<code>Stopped(Gone)</code>)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A 408, 409, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504 or 529 answer, or a stream that fails as overloaded before anything was heard,
				is tried again, twice, after what <code>retry-after</code> says (a minute at most; else half a second,
				then a second). Each try is
				logged (<code>ai.retry</code>). Once text has been passed on, nothing is tried again.
			</p>

			<Heading id="sends">What it sends</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>{"POST <base_url>/v1/messages"}</code>, with <code>anthropic-version: 2026-10-21</code>,
				<code>x-api-key</code>, and <code>"stream": true</code>: every call streams, so a long answer never
				times out (30 minutes at most). <code>output_config</code> carries the effort, and
				<code>extract!</code>'s format; <code>cache_control</code> asks for the prompt cache. It sends no
				sampling parameters (that version refuses <code>temperature</code> and the rest), no
				<code>thinking</code> (the current models think as they need to, and <code>effort</code> says how much),
				and no forced <code>tool_choice</code> (Claude Opus 5.5 refuses it).
			</p>
			<p>
				<code>effort</code> is always sent, so the model must take it. Claude Opus 4.5 and every model from the
				4.6s on do; Claude Haiku 4.5 and Sonnet 4.5 refuse it (a 400), so <code>ANTHROPIC_MODEL</code> can't
				name those.
			</p>
			<p>
				On the models that take it (<code>claude-opus-5-5</code>, <code>claude-opus-5</code>,
				<code>claude-sonnet-5-5</code>, <code>claude-fable-5-2</code>, <code>claude-fable-5-1</code>, and Mythos
				5.2 and 5.1), it sends <code>"fallbacks": "default"</code> with
				<code>anthropic-beta: server-side-fallback-2026-07-01</code>. When the model's safety classifiers
				decline a request, the API runs it again, in the same call, on the model it names for that kind of
				refusal. <code>claude-haiku-5-5</code> has no fallback, so a refusal there is <code>Refused</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="example">The example app</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>packages/ai/example</code> is a notes assistant. Its page has a chat with an assistant agent that
				hands notes to a notes keeper and meals to a nutritionist, each an agent it uses as a tool, its answers
				streamed as they are written; a button that sums up the notes (<code>ask!</code>); and a meal
				described in words, read into a typed record (<code>extract!</code>). The notes and the chats are kept
				in the server's memory (Kv).
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it shows</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>main.roc</code></td><td>the header and the plugin</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Notes.roc</code></td><td>the notes, and the three tools the notes keeper uses</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Team.roc</code></td><td>the agents: the notes keeper, the nutritionist, and the assistant that uses both as tools</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Chats.roc</code></td><td>each chat's conversation, saved and loaded</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/api/chat/[chat]/+server.roc</code></td><td>the chat: <code>post!</code> the message, then <code>events!</code> with <code>Agent.chat!</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/api/notes/summary/+server.roc</code></td><td>the summary, with <code>ask!</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/api/meal/+server.roc</code></td><td>the meal, with <code>extract!</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Ui/Chat.rocx</code></td><td>the page's chat: it posts, then listens</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Its tests (<code>example/test/ai.test.mjs</code>) run it against a stand-in for the API
				(<code>example/test/stand-in.mjs</code>), and check what the app sent as well as what it made of the
				answer. They never ask the API itself. To try the example against the API, with your key:
			</p>
			<Code source={"ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-… bin/rocx dev packages/ai/example --port 8095"} plain numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Under <code>--dev</code> it asks <code>claude-haiku-5-5</code>. One chat message to the example's team asked
				the model six times (the assistant three, the nutritionist once, the notes keeper twice): about 4,400
				tokens read, most from the cache, and 470 written, well under a tenth of a cent. The package's <code>README.md</code> is the reference. Images and documents in a
				prompt, batches, counting tokens, the API's own tools (web search, code execution) and an agent's work
				heard by its caller are not built yet; the package's <code>PROPOSALS.md</code> has them.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
